<DIV class=3Dsection>
<H1><A id=3Dintroduction class=3Dtoc-backref=20
href=3D"https://sysbio.ioc.ee/projects/f2py2e/#id38"=20
name=3Dintroduction>Introduction</A></H1>
<P>The purpose of the F2PY --<EM>Fortran to Python interface =
generator</EM>--=20
project is to provide connection between <A class=3Dreference=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/">Python</A> and Fortran languages. F2PY =
is a Python=20
extension tool for creating Python C/API modules from (handwritten or =
F2PY=20
generated) signature files (or directly from Fortran sources). The =
generated=20
extension modules facilitate:</P>
<UL class=3Dsimple>
  <LI>Calling Fortran 77/90/95, Fortran 90/95 module, and C functions =
from=20
  Python.=20
  <LI>Accessing Fortran 77 <TT class=3D"docutils literal"><SPAN=20
  class=3Dpre>COMMON</SPAN></TT> blocks and Fortran 90/95 module data =
(including=20
  allocatable arrays) from Python.=20
  <LI>Calling Python functions from Fortran or C (call-backs).=20
  <LI>Automatically handling the difference in the data storage order of =

  multi-dimensional Fortran and Numerical Python (i.e. C) arrays. =
</LI></UL>
<P>In addition, F2PY can build the generated extension modules to shared =

libraries with one command. F2PY uses the <TT class=3D"docutils =
literal"><SPAN=20
class=3Dpre>scipy_distutils</SPAN></TT> module from <A class=3Dreference =

href=3D"http://www.scipy.org/">SciPy</A> that supports number of major =
Fortran=20
compilers.</P><!-- (see `COMPILERS.txt`_ for more information). -->
<P>F2PY generated extension modules depend on <A class=3Dreference=20
href=3D"http://www.numpy.org/">NumPy</A> package that provides fast=20
multi-dimensional array language facility to Python.</P>
<DIV class=3Dsection>
<H2><A id=3Dmain-features class=3Dtoc-backref=20
href=3D"https://sysbio.ioc.ee/projects/f2py2e/#id39" =
name=3Dmain-features>Main=20
features</A></H2>
<P>Here follows a more detailed list of F2PY features:</P>
<UL>
  <LI>
  <P class=3Dfirst>F2PY scans real Fortran codes to produce the =
so-called=20
  signature files (.pyf files). The signature files contain all the =
information=20
  (function names, arguments and their types, etc.) that is needed to =
construct=20
  Python bindings to Fortran (or C) functions.</P>
  <P>The syntax of signature files is borrowed from the Fortran 90/95 =
language=20
  specification and has some F2PY specific extensions. The signature =
files can=20
  be modified to dictate how Fortran (or C) programs are called from =
Python:</P>
  <BLOCKQUOTE>
    <UL class=3Dsimple>
      <LI>F2PY solves dependencies between arguments (this is relevant =
for the=20
      order of initializing variables in extension modules).=20
      <LI>Arguments can be specified to be optional or hidden that =
simplifies=20
      calling Fortran programs from Python considerably.=20
      <LI>In principle, one can design any Python signature for a given =
Fortran=20
      function, e.g. change the order arguments, introduce auxiliary =
arguments,=20
      hide the arguments, process the arguments before passing to =
Fortran,=20
      return arguments as output of F2PY generated functions, etc.=20
  </LI></UL></BLOCKQUOTE>
  <LI>
  <P class=3Dfirst>F2PY automatically generates __doc__ strings (and =
optionally=20
  LaTeX documentation) for extension modules.</P>
  <LI>
  <P class=3Dfirst>F2PY generated functions accept arbitrary (but =
sensible) Python=20
  objects as arguments. The F2PY interface automatically takes care of=20
  type-casting and handling of non-contiguous arrays.</P>
  <LI>
  <P class=3Dfirst>The following Fortran constructs are recognized by =
F2PY:</P>
  <UL>
    <LI>
    <P class=3Dfirst>All basic Fortran types:</P><PRE =
class=3Dliteral-block>integer[ | *1 | *2 | *4 | *8 ], logical[ | *1 | *2 =
| *4 | *8 ]
integer*([ -1 | -2 | -4 | -8 ])
character[ | *(*) | *1 | *2 | *3 | ... ]
real[ | *4 | *8 | *16 ], double precision
complex[ | *8 | *16 | *32 ]
</PRE>
    <P>Negative <TT class=3D"docutils literal"><SPAN =
class=3Dpre>integer</SPAN></TT>=20
    kinds are used to wrap unsigned integers.</P>
    <LI>
    <P class=3Dfirst>Multi-dimensional arrays of all basic types with =
the=20
    following dimension specifications:</P><PRE =
class=3Dliteral-block>&lt;dim&gt; | &lt;start&gt;:&lt;end&gt; | * | :
</PRE>
    <LI>
    <P class=3Dfirst>Attributes and statements:</P><PRE =
class=3Dliteral-block>intent([ in | inout | out | hide | in,out | =
inout,out | c |
         copy | cache | callback | inplace | aux ])
dimension(&lt;dimspec&gt;)
common, parameter
allocatable
optional, required, external
depend([&lt;names&gt;])
check([&lt;C-booleanexpr&gt;])
note(&lt;LaTeX text&gt;)
usercode, callstatement, callprotoargument, threadsafe, fortranname
pymethoddef
entry
</PRE></LI></UL>
  <LI>
  <P class=3Dfirst>Because there are only little (and easily handleable) =

  differences between calling C and Fortran functions from F2PY =
generated=20
  extension modules, then F2PY is also well suited for wrapping C =
libraries to=20
  Python.</P>
  <LI>
  <P class=3Dfirst>Practice has shown that F2PY generated interfaces (to =
C or=20
  Fortran functions) are less error prone and even more efficient than=20
  handwritten extension modules. The F2PY generated interfaces are easy =
to=20
  maintain and any future optimization of F2PY generated interfaces=20
  transparently apply to extension modules by just regenerating them =
with the=20
  latest version of F2PY.</P>
  <LI>
  <P class=3Dfirst><A class=3Dreference=20
  =
href=3D"https://sysbio.ioc.ee/projects/f2py2e/usersguide/index.html">F2PY=
 Users=20
  Guide and Reference Manual</A></P></LI></UL></DIV></DIV>
<DIV class=3Dsection>
<H1><A id=3Dprerequisites class=3Dtoc-backref=20
href=3D"https://sysbio.ioc.ee/projects/f2py2e/#id40"=20
name=3Dprerequisites>Prerequisites</A></H1>
<P>F2PY requires the following software installed:</P>
<UL class=3Dsimple>
  <LI><A class=3Dreference href=3D"http://www.python.org/">Python</A> =
(versions=20
  1.5.2 or later; 2.1 and up are recommended). You must have python-dev =
package=20
  installed.=20
  <LI><A class=3Dreference href=3D"http://www.numpy.org/">NumPy</A> =
(versions 13 or=20
  later; 20.x, 21.x, 22.x, 23.x are recommended)=20
  <LI><A class=3Dreference=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.stsci.edu/resources/software_hardware/numarray">Numarr=
ay</A>=20
  (version 0.9 and up), optional, partial support.=20
  <LI>Scipy_distutils (version 0.2.2 and up are recommended) from <A=20
  class=3Dreference href=3D"http://www.scipy.org/">SciPy</A> project. =
Get it from=20
  Scipy CVS or download it below. </LI></UL>
<P>Python 1.x users also need <A class=3Dreference=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/sigs/distutils-sig/">distutils</A>.</P>
<P>Of course, to build extension modules, you'll need also working C =
and/or=20
Fortran compilers installed.</P></DIV>
<DIV class=3Dsection>
<H1><A id=3Ddownload class=3Dtoc-backref=20
href=3D"https://sysbio.ioc.ee/projects/f2py2e/#id41"=20
name=3Ddownload>Download</A></H1>
<P>You can download the sources for the latest F2PY and scipy_distutils =
releases=20
as:</P>
<UL class=3Dsimple>
  <LI><A class=3Dreference=20
  href=3D"https://sysbio.ioc.ee/projects/f2py2e/2.x/">2.x</A>/<A =
